{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates the skewness of the lotteries, specifically whether a greater portion of probability is assigned to favorable outcomes (higher monetary amounts) compared to unfavorable ones. For Lottery A: there is a 100% chance of receiving 2400 Euros, so all probability mass is on the favorable outcome (2400), making it perfectly right-skewed (no unfavorable outcomes). For Lottery B: the outcomes are 2500 Euros (33%), 2400 Euros (66%), and 0 Euros (1%). The majority of the probability (66%) is on 2400 Euros, and a small portion (1%) is on 0 Euros. Since 2500 is favorable and 0 is unfavorable, and the probability of favorable outcomes (2500 and 2400) is 33% + 66% = 99%, the favorable outcomes dominate. However, the distribution is not left-skewed — left-skew implies a longer tail on the left (lower outcomes), which is not the case here. In fact, the distribution has a peak at 2400 and a small drop to 0. This suggests that Lottery B is actually right-skewed (mass concentrated on higher values), and not left-skewed. A left-skewed lottery would have more weight on lower outcomes (unfavorable) than higher ones. Since both lotteries have most of their probability on favorable outcomes, and neither is left-skewed, the decision reason (which prefers the more left-skewed lottery) does not apply to either. Therefore, the decision reason cannot be applied to this decision problem because neither lottery is left-skewed. Thus, it is not applicable to evaluate.",
  "step_2": "The verbal report states: 'Lotterie A grantiert einen Geldgewinn.' This translates to 'Lottery A guarantees a monetary gain.' The individual acknowledges that Lottery A provides a certain gain (2400 Euros), implying that it is a safe and certain outcome. However, the report does not mention any consideration of skewness, probabilities of different outcomes, or comparisons between favorable and unfavorable outcomes. The individual does not discuss the distribution of outcomes, nor do they reference the concept of skewness, left-skew, or probability mass on favorable versus unfavorable outcomes. The justification provided is minimal and focuses only on the certainty of the gain, not on the structure of the probability distribution. Thus, no reasoning related to skewness is present in the report.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating skewness, specifically whether a lottery is left-skewed (greater probability on unfavorable outcomes), and preferring the more left-skewed one. However, neither lottery is left-skewed: Lottery A is fully right-skewed (all probability on a favorable outcome), and Lottery B has most of its probability on favorable outcomes (2400 and 2500), with only a small 1% on 0 Euros. Thus, no lottery is left-skewed, so the reason does not apply. The verbal report does not mention skewness, probability distribution, or any comparison of favorable vs. unfavorable outcomes. The individual only notes that A guarantees a gain, which is a different consideration. Since the reason is not applicable to the lotteries and is not reflected in the reported deliberation process, the individual did not use the decision reason. Therefore, the assessment is that the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

{
